  {
   "id": "Km46yM5BCwAJ",
   "from": "Mauricio Garnier-Villarreal",
   "author": "82889e926eb5165e",
   "date": "2020-01-28T19:18:04Z",
   "subject": "Re: Standard error (SE) of difference between two independent gains via lavaan",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Yes, you can define this in lavaan, with the define parameter as I added before, the define parameter EF will be estimated with the respective SE<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><div>Lets say the parameters are named as such in the lavaan model<br></div><div>- MD1 = Mean.dif<font size=\"1\">group 1</font><br></div><div>- VR1 (variance)= SD.dif<font size=\"1\">group 1</font><br></div><div>- MD2 = Mean.dif\r\n\r\n<font size=\"1\">group 2</font><br></div><div>- VR2 (variance)= SD.dif<font size=\"1\">group 2</font></div><div><br></div><div>EFFECT SIZE = [Mean.dif<font size=\"1\">group 1</font>/SD.dif<font size=\"1\">group 1</font>] − [Mean.dif\r\n\r\n<font size=\"1\">group 2</font> /SD.dif<font size=\"1\">group 2</font>] ### this translate into the define parameter EF<br></div>EF := (MD1/sqrt(VR1)) - (MD2/sqrt(VR2))</div><div><br></div><br></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Tuesday, January 28, 2020 at 10:55:49 AM UTC-6, Reza Norouzian wrote:</div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Terrence;<br><div><br></div><div>I appreciate your valuable input, thank you! Just to make sure, the effect size I&#39;m trying to get the SE for is subtraction of two well-established within-group effect sizes (I&#39;m aware there are also alternatives). This effect size appears in Table 1 (last row), p. 114 of <a href=\"https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/d88e/7804b7df89d229fd5db7a1c567b94ff18f33.pdf\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/d88e/7804b7df89d229fd5db7a1c567b94ff18f33.pdf&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422446673000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3YBU2YAikRaYU4CX4pM3Oq\"><b>this article</b></a>.</div><div><br></div><div>But aside from that, can we define either my version or your suggested version in lavaan to get their SE?</div><div><br></div><div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/b3a0be08ecee0/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1&amp;authuser=0\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"margin-right:0px\" width=\"687\" height=\"450\"><br></div></div></div><br></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\">On Tue, Jan 28, 2020 at 3:18 AM Terrence Jorgensen &lt;<a rel=\"nofollow\">tjorge...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Internally standardizing (i.e., within each group separately) is problematic.  Suppose the pre-post difference is the same in both groups.  The group difference would be zero, and a standardized version of that effect (i.e., the group-by-time interaction) should also be zero.  But if group 1&#39;s SD.dif is smaller/larger than group 2&#39;s SD.dif, then the difference in (internally) standardized means would be nonzero.  Large differences in SD.dif between groups would make your effect appear large, even when there is no difference between groups.  </div><div><br></div><div>The only way standardization works is to use a common norm. So you should probably pool SD.dif as well.  Using the principle behind the method Mike described, you would need to allow for group sizes to differ when you calculate SD.dif_pooled (e.g., see the formula for pooled &quot;<i>s</i>&quot; under Cohen&#39;s <i>d</i>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effect_size#Cohen&#39;s_d\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effect_size%23Cohen&#39;s_d&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422446673000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0VFPgJZ8LynJtq-2YrReiG\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effect_size#Cohen&#39;s_d</a> )</div><div><br></div><div><div><div>Terrence D. Jorgensen</div><div>Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics</div><div>Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam</div><div><a href=\"http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792422446673000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2kusH4fT1c1tf7JDVqsuJQ\">http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen</a></div></div><div><br></div></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;lavaan&quot; group.<br></blockquote></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
